 interval $t\\in(-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of  $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain a total of four candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (0,-2), \\quad (2,0), \\quad (0,2).\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(x,y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,2)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f(x,y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(0,-2),$  and $(0,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124280 :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-9$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $27$ | $3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $12$ | $27$ | $56$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. It is one-to-one\n2. It is many-to-one\n3. It is not a function" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$56$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. It is one-to-one\n2. It is many-to-one\n3. It is not a function"] [:db/cas 17592186124280 :question/worked-solution "Remember that\n\n- a mapping diagram represents a *function* if every element in the domain maps to exactly one element in the range,\n- a function is *one-to-one* if every element in the range is paired with one (and only one) element in the domain, and\n- a function is *many-to-one* if some element in the range is paired with multiple elements in the domain.\n\nIn the given table, every element in the domain maps to exactly one element in the range, so it represents a function.\n\nThe element $g(x)=27$ in the range is paired with multiple elements ($x=-9$ and $x=9$) in the domain, so the function is many-to-one but not one-to-one.\n\n| **$x$** | $\\color{red}-9$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $\\color{blue}27$ | $3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $12$ | $\\color{blue}27$ | $56$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that\n\n- a mapping diagram represents a *function* if every element in the domain maps to exactly one element in the range,\n- a function is *one-to-one* if every element in the range is paired with one (and only one) element in the domain, and\n- a function is *many-to-one* if some element in the range is paired with multiple elements in the domain.\n\nIn the given table, every element in the domain maps to exactly one element in the range, so it represents a function.\n\nThe element $g(x)=27$ in the range is paired with multiple elements ($x=-9$ and $x=9$) in the domain, so the function is many-to-one but not one-to-one.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$56$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186124332 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$$\n\\displaystyle A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\det  ( A - \\lambda  I ) &  = 0 \\\\ ∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( 1 - \\lambda  ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ - ( - 1 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ + ( - 2 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 - \\lambda  \\\\ - 2 & - 2\\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 17 \\lambda  - 11 ) + ( 3 + \\lambda  ) - 2 ( 4 - 2 \\lambda  ) &  = 0 \\\\ - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 22 \\lambda  - 16 &  = 0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3} + 5 {\\lambda }^{2} - 22 \\lambda  + 16 &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16\\mathbin{:}$ \n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16\n$$\n\nLet's pick $\\lambda=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( 1 {)}^{3} + 5 ( 1 {)}^{2} - 22 ( 1 ) + 16 & \\displaystyle  = 0.\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=2,$ and $\\,\\lambda_3=-8.$" "First, we find the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$$\n\\displaystyle A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\det  ( A - \\lambda  I ) &  = 0 \\\\ ∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( 1 - \\lambda  ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ - ( - 1 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ + ( - 2 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 - \\lambda  \\\\ - 2 & - 2\\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 17 \\lambda  - 11 ) + ( 3 + \\lambda  ) - 2 ( 4 - 2 \\lambda  ) &  = 0 \\\\ - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 22 \\lambda  - 16 &  = 0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3} + 5 {\\lambda }^{2} - 22 \\lambda  + 16 &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16\\mathbin{:}$ \n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16\n$$\n\nLet's pick $\\lambda=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( 1 {)}^{3} + 5 ( 1 {)}^{2} - 22 ( 1 ) + 16 & \\displaystyle  = 0.\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EEEEEE\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=2,$ and $\\,\\lambda_3=-8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124347 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\:\\:x^{10}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^2-1\\:$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^{12}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^{10}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\:\\:x^{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2-1\\:$</td>\n<td colspan=\"7\" data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$x^{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$x^{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\">$2x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124353 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Public Transport </b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Public Transport</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n$$\nP = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.75\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>Public Transport </b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>Public Transport</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n$$\nP = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.75\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124354 :question/problem "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Sugar $A$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Sugar $B$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Sugar $A$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Sugar $B$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change."] [:db/cas 17592186124421 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\right)}^{2}+2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\right)}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\right)}^{2}+2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\right)}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, (x,y) \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, f(x,y) \\,$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, -3 \\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 3 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124424 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}1 = 2 \\lambda  x \\\\ 1 = 2 \\lambda  y \\\\ 1 = 8 \\lambda  z\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2 \\lambda }} \\\\ y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2 \\lambda }} \\\\ z = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8 \\lambda }}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\left(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}\\right) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}1 = 2 \\lambda  x \\\\ 1 = 2 \\lambda  y \\\\ 1 = 8 \\lambda  z\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2 \\lambda }} \\\\ y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2 \\lambda }} \\\\ z = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8 \\lambda }}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\left(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}\\right) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, (x,y,z) \\,$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, -9 \\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 9 \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124427 :question/problem "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase. \n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Laptop</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Desktop</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Laptop</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Desktop</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase. \n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?"] [:db/cas 17592186124774 :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20\\%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70\\%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Cities</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Suburbs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Cities</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Suburbs</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThat is, $20\\%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70\\%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?"] [:db/cas 17592186124775 :question/problem "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40\\%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Museum</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Station</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Gym</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Museum</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Station</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Gym</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor example, $40\\%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?"] [:db/cas 17592186124788 :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of cars: Standard, Sports, and Deluxe. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of cars each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Standard** | **Sports** | **Deluxe** |\n| To: | **Standard** | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Sports** | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Deluxe** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nFor example, $20\\%$ of Standard customers switch to Sports each year, and $30\\%$ of Sports customers switch to Deluxe.\n\nCurrently, $500000$ customers use Standard, $400000$ customers use Sports and $100000$ customers use Deluxe. How many customers will use Sports in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "A certain company produces three types of cars: Standard, Sports, and Deluxe. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of cars each year:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Standard</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Sports</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Deluxe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Standard</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Sports</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Deluxe</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor example, $20\\%$ of Standard customers switch to Sports each year, and $30\\%$ of Sports customers switch to Deluxe.\n\nCurrently, $500000$ customers use Standard, $400000$ customers use Sports and $100000$ customers use Deluxe. How many customers will use Sports in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186124924 :question/problem "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40\\%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30\\%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Phone $A$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Phone $B$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Phone $A$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Phone $B$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThat is, $40\\%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30\\%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change."] [:db/cas 17592186124934 :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $7$ | $5$ | $3$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. It is one-to-one\n2. It is many-to-one\n3. It is not a function" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. It is one-to-one\n2. It is many-to-one\n3. It is not a function"] [:db/cas 17592186124997 :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. It is one-to-one\n2. It is many-to-one\n3. It is not a function" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. It is one-to-one\n2. It is many-to-one\n3. It is not a function"] [:db/cas 17592186125759 :tutorial/content "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><b></b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples."] [:db/cas 17592186125800 :tutorial/content "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.94$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.03$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.06$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.97$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nP={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_0={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nP\\mathbf{x}_0\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{array} \\right]}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nThe resulting vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nP\\mathbf{x}_1\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{array} \\right]}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nThe resulting vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_2= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside." "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">To:</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.94$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.03$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.06$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.97$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nP={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_0={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nP\\mathbf{x}_0\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{array} \\right]}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nThe resulting vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nP\\mathbf{x}_1\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{array} \\right]}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nThe resulting vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_2= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside."] [:db/cas 17592186125832 :tutorial/content "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><b></b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186125861 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Label</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Name</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Addition is Commutative</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Addition is Associative</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space."] [:db/cas 17592186125892 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n𝒇'(x,y) = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 x {e}^{ {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}} & - 2 y {e}^{ {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n𝒇'(x,y) = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 x {e}^{ {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}} & - 2 y {e}^{ {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(x,y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,-1)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f(x,y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1/e$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1/e$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125894 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(\\pm 1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, \\pm 1)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125898 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table of special sets.\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Equivalent To |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\emptyset$ | The empty set, $\\{\\}$ |  |\n| $\\mathbb{N}$ | The set of natural numbers, $\\{1,2,3,...\\}$ |  |\n| $\\mathbb{N}_0$ | The set of natural numbers and zero,  $\\{0,1,2,3,...\\}$ |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}$ | The set of integers, $\\{0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3,...\\}$ |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}^+$ | The set of positive integers, $\\{1,2,3,...\\}.$ | $\\mathbb N$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ | The set of positive integers and zero, $\\{0,1,2,3,...\\}$ | $\\mathbb N_0$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ | The set of negative integers, $\\{-1,-2,-3,...\\}$ |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}_0^-$ | The set of negative integers and zero, $\\{0,-1,-2,-3,...\\}$ |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}$ | The set of rational numbers |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}^+$ | The set of positive rational numbers |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}_0^+$ | The set of positive rational numbers and zero |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ | The set of negative rational numbers |  |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}_0^-$ | The set of negative rational numbers and zero |  |\n| $\\mathbb{R}$ | The set of real numbers | $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ |\n| $\\mathbb{R}^+$ | The set of positive real numbers | $x\\in (0,\\infty)$ |\n| $\\mathbb{R}_0^+$ | The set of positive real numbers and zero | $x\\in [0,\\infty)$ |\n| $\\mathbb{R}^-$ | The set of negative real numbers | $x\\in (-\\infty,0)$ |\n| $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ | The set of negative real numbers and zero | $x\\in (-\\infty,0]$ |\n\nAs noted in the table, the following sets are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbb Z^+$ and $\\mathbb N$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z_0^+$ and $\\mathbb N_0$\n\n- $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$ is the interval containing all real numbers, the same as $\\mathbb R.$\n\n- $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ is the interval containing all positive real numbers, the same as $\\mathbb R^+.$\n\n- $x\\in[0,\\infty)$ is the interval containing all positive real numbers and zero, the same as $\\mathbb R_0^+.$\n\n- $x\\in(-\\infty,0)$ is the interval containing all negative real numbers, the same as $\\mathbb R^-.$\n\n- $x\\in(-\\infty,0]$ is the interval containing all negative real numbers and zero, the same as $\\mathbb R_0^-.$" "Let's recall the table of special sets.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Meaning</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Equivalent To</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\emptyset$</td>\n<td>The empty set, $\\{\\}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{N}$</td>\n<td>The set of natural numbers, $\\{1,2,3,...\\}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{N}_0$</td>\n<td>The set of natural numbers and zero,  $\\{0,1,2,3,...\\}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Z}$</td>\n<td>The set of integers, $\\{0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3,...\\}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Z}^+$</td>\n<td>The set of positive integers, $\\{1,2,3,...\\}.$</td>\n<td>$\\mathbb N$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$</td>\n<td>The set of positive integers and zero, $\\{0,1,2,3,...\\}$</td>\n<td>$\\mathbb N_0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Z}^-$</td>\n<td>The set of negative integers, $\\{-1,-2,-3,...\\}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Z}_0^-$</td>\n<td>The set of negative integers and zero, $\\{0,-1,-2,-3,...\\}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Q}$</td>\n<td>The set of rational numbers</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Q}^+$</td>\n<td>The set of positive rational numbers</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Q}_0^+$</td>\n<td>The set of positive rational numbers and zero</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Q}^-$</td>\n<td>The set of negative rational numbers</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{Q}_0^-$</td>\n<td>The set of negative rational numbers and zero</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{R}$</td>\n<td>The set of real numbers</td>\n<td>$x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbb{R}^+$</td>\n<td>The set of positive real numbers</td>\n<td>$x\\in (0,\\inft