">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186128370 :answer/value "| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $4$ | $2$ |" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186128874 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |" "If $f(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186128967 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2x}{x - 4} & < x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{x - 4} - x & < 0 \\\\ (2x - x(x - 4))/(x - 4) & < 0 \\\\ \\frac{-x^{2} + 6x}{x - 4} & < 0 \\\\ (x(6 - x))/(x - 4) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x(6-x)}{x-4}\n$$\n\nSo, we're solving the inequality $f(x) < 0$.\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x=6$. Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=4$. The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,0), \\quad (0,4), \\quad (4,6), \\quad (6, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6, \\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $6-x$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n| $x-4$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(0, 4)$ and $(6,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) < 0$ is\n$x \\in (0,4) \\cup (6, \\infty)$." "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2x}{x - 4} & < x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{x - 4} - x & < 0 \\\\ (2x - x(x - 4))/(x - 4) & < 0 \\\\ \\frac{-x^{2} + 6x}{x - 4} & < 0 \\\\ (x(6 - x))/(x - 4) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x(6-x)}{x-4}\n$$\n\nSo, we're solving the inequality $f(x) < 0$.\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x=6$. Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=4$. The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,0), \\quad (0,4), \\quad (4,6), \\quad (6, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,4)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(4,6)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(6, \\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6-x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(0, 4)$ and $(6,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) < 0$ is\n$x \\in (0,4) \\cup (6, \\infty)$."] [:db/cas 17592186128968 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{x + 1} & \\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x + 1} - 3 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x + 1} - (3(x + 1))/(x + 1) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x - 3(x + 1))/(x + 1) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{x - 3x - 3}{x + 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{-2x - 3}{x + 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2x + 3}{x + 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{2x + 3}{x + 1} & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2x+3}{x+1}\n$$\n\nSo, we're now solving the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nAlso, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$. The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac32\\right), \\quad \\left(-\\dfrac32, -1\\right), \\quad (-1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac32\\right)$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac32,-1\\right)$ | $(-1, \\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nTo solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac32\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(-1, \\infty)$.\n\nSince our inequality is non-strict, we must remember that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution. Therefore, we conclude that the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac32 \\right] \\cup (-1, \\infty)\n$$" "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{x + 1} & \\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x + 1} - 3 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x + 1} - (3(x + 1))/(x + 1) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x - 3(x + 1))/(x + 1) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{x - 3x - 3}{x + 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{-2x - 3}{x + 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2x + 3}{x + 1} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{2x + 3}{x + 1} & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2x+3}{x+1}\n$$\n\nSo, we're now solving the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nAlso, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$. The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac32\\right), \\quad \\left(-\\dfrac32, -1\\right), \\quad (-1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac32\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\dfrac32,-1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-1, \\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2x+3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac32\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(-1, \\infty)$.\n\nSince our inequality is non-strict, we must remember that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution. Therefore, we conclude that the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac32 \\right] \\cup (-1, \\infty)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128969 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x - 2}{x + 2} & > \\frac{x}{x - 2} \\\\ \\frac{x - 2}{x + 2} - \\frac{x}{x - 2} & > 0 \\\\ ((x - 2)^{2} - x(x + 2))/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0 \\\\ (x^{2} - 4x + 4 - x^{2} - 2x)/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0 \\\\ (4 - 6x)/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0 \\\\ (2(2 - 3x))/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2(2-3x)}{(x+2)(x-2)}\n$$\n\nSo, we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0$.\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nAlso, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. The root and asymptotes divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad \\left(-2,\\frac{2}{3}\\right), \\quad \\left(\\frac{2}{3},2\\right), \\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $\\left(-2,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2-3x$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x) > 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-2,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) > 0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty,-2)\\cup\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)\n$$" "Here, we have a rational inequality that is non-zero on one side. So, we rewrite the inequality to give zero on one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x - 2}{x + 2} & > \\frac{x}{x - 2} \\\\ \\frac{x - 2}{x + 2} - \\frac{x}{x - 2} & > 0 \\\\ ((x - 2)^{2} - x(x + 2))/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0 \\\\ (x^{2} - 4x + 4 - x^{2} - 2x)/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0 \\\\ (4 - 6x)/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0 \\\\ (2(2 - 3x))/((x + 2)(x - 2)) & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed using the sign table method. First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2(2-3x)}{(x+2)(x-2)}\n$$\n\nSo, we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0$.\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nAlso, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. The root and asymptotes divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad \\left(-2,\\frac{2}{3}\\right), \\quad \\left(\\frac{2}{3},2\\right), \\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-2,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2-3x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x) > 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-2,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) > 0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty,-2)\\cup\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3},2\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129019 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y),$ shown in the table below, and the cumulative distribution function $F(y),$ compute $F(2).$\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y),$ shown in the table below, and the cumulative distribution function $F(y),$ compute $F(2).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129020 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P\\left( 2 \\leq  Y <     5 \\right).$\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ | $1$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P\\left( 2 \\leq  Y <     5 \\right).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129021 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=5).$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ | $0.50$ | $0.90$ | $1.0$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=5).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.50$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129022 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X \\gt 2).$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.8$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X \\gt 2).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129037 :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = -3xy^2.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y > 0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |" "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = -3xy^2.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y > 0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y <                0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x<         0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x> 0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129037 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-3x$ | $y^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/74/743df5f5c9232346866a01aed9afa3d41fff0f61bf7812b7ae5db9a43efe59f4.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Test point</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-3x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y^2$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Top-Left</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Top-Right</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bottom-Left</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bottom-Right</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/74/743df5f5c9232346866a01aed9afa3d41fff0f61bf7812b7ae5db9a43efe59f4.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y > 0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y <                0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x<         0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x> 0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129038 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-6x^2$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/17/17dd9e10234b6a69123241a882e267d95d505d1944d7c31ef563f9ac06b990af.png)" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Test point</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-6x^2$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Top-Left</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Top-Right</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bottom-Left</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bottom-Right</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/17/17dd9e10234b6a69123241a882e267d95d505d1944d7c31ef563f9ac06b990af.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186129075 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129077 :question/problem "The functions $f$ and $g$ are defined by the tables below. Find the value of $(f \\circ g)(4)$.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ |  | $x$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $-1$ |  | $f(x)$ | $5$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-13$ |" "The functions $f$ and $g$ are defined by the tables below. Find the value of $(f \\circ g)(4)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-13$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129417 :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=-2xy.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=-2xy.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y>0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y<0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x<0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x>0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129420 :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y>0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=