 data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129883 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(4<X\\le 6).$\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(4<X\\le 6).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129884 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129885 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X>4).$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X>4).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186129886 :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $T$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(t)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(T \\ge 9)$.\n\n| $t$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(t)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" "Given that the discrete random variable $T$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(t)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(T \\ge 9)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(t)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186130345 :tutorial/content "To plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we first create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n\nThen, we draw the graph by plotting the corresponding $(x,y)$ pairs and connecting them.\n\n![](images/3e/3eea82487a56147789d1c383c21e37c447dc94d735de094acd80183894462a38.png)\n\nThis type of curve is called a **rectangular hyperbola.**" "To plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we first create a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\dfrac{1}{x}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{undefined}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we draw the graph by plotting the corresponding $(x,y)$ pairs and connecting them.\n\n![](images/3e/3eea82487a56147789d1c383c21e37c447dc94d735de094acd80183894462a38.png)\n\nThis type of curve is called a **rectangular hyperbola.**"] [:db/cas 17592186130346 :tutorial/content "Let's now plot the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Once again, we first create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/9a/9af11caef6739f83d32e0190ddc89c98b8e0150aa430184ee5e234a7febe8d8a.png)\n\nNotice that $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is the reflection of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ over the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/50/507a0a5cf7c2a1871efd76092c5164424ce17f3425c1bfc33df89cecefa81803.png)" "Let's now plot the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Once again, we first create a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-\\dfrac{1}{x}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{undefined}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/9a/9af11caef6739f83d32e0190ddc89c98b8e0150aa430184ee5e234a7febe8d8a.png)\n\nNotice that $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is the reflection of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ over the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/50/507a0a5cf7c2a1871efd76092c5164424ce17f3425c1bfc33df89cecefa81803.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186130369 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ First, we create a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- The domain  is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The range  is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large." "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ First, we create a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- The domain  is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The range  is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large."] [:db/cas 17592186130416 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x,y),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2y.\n$$\n\nThis is a function of two variables, $x$ and $y.$ The function is positive for some values of $(x,y)$ and negative for others. How can we determine those values of $x$ and $y$ for which the function is positive, and those for which the function is negative?\n\nTo answer this question, we first solve $f(x,y) = 0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot y=0\\, & ⟹\\,2{x}^{2}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equations $2x^2 = 0$ and $y = 0$ gives the solutions $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These solutions divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nOur task now is to determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region. To do this, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nWe compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region by calculating the sign at each test point. We record our results in a sign table.\n\n|  | Test point | $2x^2$ | $y$ | $f(x,y) = 2x^2\\cdot y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/7c/7c851189a040934728e1b312706b4727c141cc678a4be870b6057e281be6e0fd.png)\n\nWe can also record our findings using a simplified sign table, like the one below.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |" "Consider the function $f(x,y),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2y.\n$$\n\nThis is a function of two variables, $x$ and $y.$ The function is positive for some values of $(x,y)$ and negative for others. How can we determine those values of $x$ and $y$ for which the function is positive, and those for which the function is negative?\n\nTo answer this question, we first solve $f(x,y) = 0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot y=0\\, & ⟹\\,2{x}^{2}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equations $2x^2 = 0$ and $y = 0$ gives the solutions $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These solutions divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nOur task now is to determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region. To do this, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nWe compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region by calculating the sign at each test point. We record our results in a sign table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Test point</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2x^2$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y) = 2x^2\\cdot y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Top-Left</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Top-Right</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bottom-Left</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bottom-Right</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/7c/7c851189a040934728e1b312706b4727c141cc678a4be870b6057e281be6e0fd.png)\n\nWe can also record our findings using a simplified sign table, like the one below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y > 0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y <                0$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x<         0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x> 0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186133478 :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$"] [:db/cas 17592186133613 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$$\n(X^T \\! X )^{-1} =\n\\dfrac{1}{104}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\nX^T \\mathbf{y} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}27 \\\\ - 26\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$$\n(X^T \\! X )^{-1} =\n\\dfrac{1}{104}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\nX^T \\mathbf{y} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}27 \\\\ - 26\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*"] [:db/cas 17592186133614 :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n$$\n(X^T \\! X )^{-1} =\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}23 & - 6 \\\\ - 6 & 2\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\nX^T \\mathbf{y} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Mass $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Stretch $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n$$\n(X^T \\! X )^{-1} =\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}23 & - 6 \\\\ - 6 & 2\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\nX^T \\mathbf{y} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*"] [:db/cas 17592186133615 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T\\!X)^{-1} X^T\\!\\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T\\!X)^{-1} X^T\\!\\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context."] [:db/cas 17592186133615 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."] [:db/cas 17592186133616 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n𝜷 = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{y} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n𝜷 = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{y} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133617 :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$7$** | **$6.5$** | **$6$** | **$5$** | **$3$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$7$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6.5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context."] [:db/cas 17592186133617 :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}7$** | **$\\color{purple}6.5$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}5$** | **$\\color{purple}3$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $\\color{red}15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n$$\nX= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad \n \\mathbf{y}=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}7$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}6.5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}3$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n$$\nX= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad \n \\mathbf{y}=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133618 :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$-8$** | **$-2$** | **$6$** | **$15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$8$** | **$6$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $60$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$15$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context."] [:db/cas 17592186133618 :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}-8$** | **$\\color{purple}-2$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$\\color{green}8$** | **$\\color{green}6$** | **$\\color{green}4$** | **$\\color{green}2$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}10$ | $\\color{red}20$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}60$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny= \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have \n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & - 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{y}= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}-8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}-2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{purple}15$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{green}8$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{green}6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{green}4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{green}2$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}60$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny= \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have \n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & - 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{y}= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133619 :question/worked-solution "If $q(2) = 0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x).$ To find the remaining zeros, we need to find the other factors of $q(x).$\n\n So $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $q(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $q(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-18$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3+3x^2-18x+8}{x-2} = 2x^2+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x^2+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to $0$ and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0\\, & 2x-1 & =0\\, & x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\, & x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\, & x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $q(2) = 0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x).$ To find the remaining zeros, we need to find the other factors of $q(x).$\n\n So $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $q(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $q(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3+3x^2-18x+8}{x-2} = 2x^2+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x^2+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to $0$ and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0\\, & 2x-1 & =0\\, & x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\, & x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\, & x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133620 :question/worked-solution "To find the remaining roots, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x).$ So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+2x^2-x-2}{x+2} = x^2-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x^2-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x+1)(x-1).\n$$" "To find the remaining roots, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x).$ So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+2x^2-x-2}{x+2} = x^2-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x^2-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3+2x^2-x-2 = (x+2) (x+1)(x-1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133623 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2x+1)(x-2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $x=2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right), \\quad \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2},2 \\right), \\quad (2,7), \\quad (7, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac12,2\\right)$ | $(2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-7$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)> 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $\\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right)$ and $(7,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right) \\cup (7,\\infty).$" "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2x+1)(x-2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $x=2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right), \\quad \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2},2 \\right), \\quad (2,7), \\quad (7, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac12\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\dfrac12,2\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,7)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(7,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2x+1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)> 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $\\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right)$ and $(7,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right) \\cup (7,\\infty).$"] [:db/cas 17592186133624 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+3)-(x+3)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+3)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2(x+3)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n( -\\infty,-3 ), \\quad ( -3,0 ), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq  0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-3, 0)$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) \\leq  0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in [-3,0]\\cup [0,1] =  [-3,1].\n$$" "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+3)-(x+3)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+3)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2(x+3)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n( -\\infty,-3 ), \\quad ( -3,0 ), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-3,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq  0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-3, 0)$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) \\leq  0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in [-3,0]\\cup [0,1] =  [-3,1].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133626 :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2{x}^{2}-3) & <5x \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3)-5x & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3-5) & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-8) & <0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-4) & <0 \\\\ 2x(x+2)(x-2) & <0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) < 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x = 0,$ and $x = 2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad  (-2,0),\\quad (0,2),\\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0,2).$" "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2{x}^{2}-3) & <5x \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3)-5x & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3-5) & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-8) & <0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-4) & <0 \\\\ 2x(x+2)(x-2) & <0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) < 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x = 0,$ and $x = 2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad  (-2,0),\\quad (0,2),\\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-2,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186133627 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right using a table of values.\n\n|  |  | $\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Approaching $3$ (from the *right*) |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$x$** | $3$ | $\\cdots$ | $3.0001$ | $3.001$ | $3.01$ | $3.1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | undefined | $\\cdots$ | $-1999.96$ | $-199.96$ | $-19.96$ | $-1.96$ |\n|  |  | $\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Decreasing to $\\color{red}-\\infty$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *negative*, and they *decrease* as we approach $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{red}{-\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow 3^+$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{red}{-\\infty}}\n$$" "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td 