\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$120$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context."] [:db/cas 17592186133635 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** | **$\\color{blue}6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}70$ | $\\color{red}120$ | $\\color{red}100$ | $\\color{red}80$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ 70 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 100 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}120$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}80$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}40 \\\\ 70 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 100 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."] [:db/cas 17592186133640 :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $20$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-2$ | $12$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-6$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20}{x+2} = x^2 - 6x + 10,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\nb^2 - 4ac = (-6)^2 - 4(1)(10) = -4 <           0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$" "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20}{x+2} = x^2 - 6x + 10,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\nb^2 - 4ac = (-6)^2 - 4(1)(10) = -4 <           0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133641 :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2}{x+1} = x^2 - 2x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1) (x^2 - 2x -2).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = b^2 - 4ac = (-2)^2 - 4(1)(-2) = 12 >     0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1)(x^2 - 2x - 2).\n$$\n\n**Important Note**: Although the quadratic polynomial $(x^2 - 2x - 2)$ does not factor over the rational numbers, it *does* have real roots, because $\\mathcal D > 0.$ Therefore, if we were asked to find all of the real roots of $f(x),$ we would need to solve the equations \n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x^2 - 2x - 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe quadratic equation could be solved using the quadratic formula or by completing the square." "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2}{x+1} = x^2 - 2x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1) (x^2 - 2x -2).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = b^2 - 4ac = (-2)^2 - 4(1)(-2) = 12 >     0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1)(x^2 - 2x - 2).\n$$\n\n**Important Note**: Although the quadratic polynomial $(x^2 - 2x - 2)$ does not factor over the rational numbers, it *does* have real roots, because $\\mathcal D > 0.$ Therefore, if we were asked to find all of the real roots of $f(x),$ we would need to solve the equations \n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x^2 - 2x - 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe quadratic equation could be solved using the quadratic formula or by completing the square."] [:db/cas 17592186133664 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $0$ from the *left*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left using a table of values.\n\n|  | Approaching $0$ (from the *left*) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$x$** | $-0.1$ | $-0.01$ | $-0.001$ | $-0.0001$ | $\\cdots$ | $0$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $9.18$ | $99.02$ | $999.00$ | $9\\,999.00$ | $\\cdots$ | undefined |\n|  | Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive*, and they *increase* as we approach $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow 0^-$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$" "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $0$ from the *left*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\">Approaching $0$ (from the <em>left</em>) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.001$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.0001$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9.18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$99.02$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$999.00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9\\,999.00$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive*, and they *increase* as we approach $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow 0^-$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133666 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)^2(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+2)^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\leq 0$ for all $x \\in \\{-2\\} \\cup[0, 1].$" "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)^2(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-2,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(x+2)^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\leq 0$ for all $x \\in \\{-2\\} \\cup[0, 1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186133718 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186133718 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}-6$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}-3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}-1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$\\color{blue}4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."] [:db/cas 17592186133720 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-4$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right using a table of values.\n\n|  |  | $\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Approaching $-4$ (from the *right*) |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$x$** | $-4$ | $\\cdots$ | $-3.9999$ | $-3.999$ | $-3.99$ | $-3.9$ |\n| $f(x)$ | undefined | $\\cdots$ | $10\\,001$ | $1\\,001$ | $101$ | $11$ |\n|  |  | $\\large\\longleftarrow\\quad$ Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive*, and they *increase* as we approach $x=-4$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow -4^+$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$" "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-4$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\inft