rrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow -4^+$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133721 :question/worked-solution "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $6$ from the *left*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=6$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left using a table of values.\n\n|  | Approaching $6$ (from the *left*) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$x$** | $5.9$ | $5.99$ | $5.999$ | $5.9999$ | $\\cdots$ | $6$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $9$ | $99$ | $999$ | $9\\,999$ | $\\cdots$ | undefined |\n|  | Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive*, and they *increase* as we approach $x=6$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow 6^-$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$" "We wish to determine the behavior of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $6$ from the *left*.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=6$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left.\n\nWe can see how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left using a table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\">Approaching $6$ (from the <em>left</em>) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.9999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9\\,999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">Increasing to $\\color{blue}\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that all the values of $f(x)$ in our table are *positive*, and they *increase* as we approach $x=6$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\infty}}$ as $x\\rightarrow 6^-$, which means that\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{\\infty}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186134106 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186134107 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1^{2} & 1^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2^{2} & 2^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3^{2} & 3^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & 4^{2} & 4^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5^{2} & 5^{3}\\end{bmatrix}, β = \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1^{2} & 1^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2^{2} & 2^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3^{2} & 3^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & 4^{2} & 4^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5^{2} & 5^{3}\\end{bmatrix}, β = \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186134108 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [:db/cas 17592186134109 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $9$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $-12$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186134111 :question/problem "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Age $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Height $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [:db/cas 17592186134113 :question/problem "| $x_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$12$** | **$15$** | **$18$** | **$22$** |\n| $x_{3}$ | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$3$** | **$7$** | **$12$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x_{1} + β_{2}x_{2} + β_{3}x_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$10$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$12$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$15$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$18$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$22$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$-2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$0$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$7$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$12$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x_{1} + β_{2}x_{2} + β_{3}x_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186134114 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | *