sign matrix $X$ in this context?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186134391 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186134392 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186134470 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x ∈ [0, 5]$. Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $8$ | $7$ | $14$ | $12$ | $18$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x) = 13$?" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x ∈ [0, 5]$. Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x) = 13$?"] [:db/cas 17592186134516 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x ∈ [0, 3]$. Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $4$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$?" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x ∈ [0, 3]$. Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$?"] [:db/cas 17592186135062 :tutorial/content "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" "Consider the following data set:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$110$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$120$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$130$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186135063 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Goods $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**."] [:db/cas 17592186135065 :tutorial/content "If we're given a root $r$ of a polynomial $p(x),$ then the factor theorem tells us that $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x).$ \n\nFor example, if we're given that $x=2$ is a root of the polynomial  $p(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2,$ then the factor theorem tells us that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBut what if we want to factor the polynomial completely? How can we find the other factors?\n\nThe trick is to use division. Because $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $p(x).$ So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nNotice that the remainder turned out to be zero. Whenever we divide a polynomial by one of its factors, we *always* get a remainder of zero.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2}=x^2-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3-2x^2-x+2 = (x-2) (x^2-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3-2x^2-x+2 = (x-2) (x-1) (x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=2, \\qquad x=1, \\qquad x=-1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this method and the factor theorem to factor polynomials and solve polynomial equations. Let's try a few more examples!" "If we're given a root $r$ of a polynomial $p(x),$ then the factor theorem tells us that $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x).$ \n\nFor example, if we're given that $x=2$ is a root of the polynomial  $p(x)=x^3-2x^2-x+2,$ then the factor theorem tells us that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBut what if we want to factor the polynomial completely? How can we find the other factors?\n\nThe trick is to use division. Because $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $p(x).$ So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$x^0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"solid\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$\\color{gray}\\downarrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that the remainder turned out to be zero. Whenever we divide a polynomial by one of its factors, we *always* get a remainder of zero.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-x+2}{x-2}=x^2-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3-2x^2-x+2 = (x-2) (x^2-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^3-2x^2-x+2 = (x-2) (x-1) (x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=2, \\qquad x=1, \\qquad x=-1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this method and the factor theorem to factor polynomials and solve polynomial equations. Let's try a few more examples!"] [:db/cas 17592186135067 :tutorial/content "Let's use the sign table method to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-2)(x-3) \\leq 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) =(x+2)(x-2)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nNext, we find the solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0,$ which are $x=-2, x=2,$ and $x=3.$ Since the inequality is not strict, these values *are* solutions to the inequality.\nMoreover, these roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-2 \\right), \\qquad (-2,2), \\qquad (2,3),  \\qquad (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nOn each of these intervals, the factors of the polynomial are either always positive or always negative. We can find out which by filling a sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $x-3$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $f(x)$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTo fill out the sign table, we have to figure out the sign of each factor on each of the intervals. We can do this by finding the sign of each factor at a chosen test point in each of the intervals:\n\nIn the interval $(-\\infty, -2),$ we can choose the test point $x_1=-3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-3+2=-1<0, \\\\ x-2 & =-3-2=-5<0, \\\\ x-3 & =-3-3=-6<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso all three factors are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty, -2).$\n\nBy applying the same method for the other intervals and placing the signs in the sign table, we get the following table.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTo fill in the final row, we compute the product of the signs in each of the columns. This gives the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(2,3).$\n\nFinally, since this is an \"or equal to\" inequality, the roots of $f(x),$ namely $x=-2, x=2,$ and $x=3$ are also solutions to the inequality. Therefore, we have that $f(x) \\leq 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2] \\cup [2,3].$" "Let's use the sign table method to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-2)(x-3) \\leq 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) =(x+2)(x-2)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nNext, we find the solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0,$ which are $x=-2, x=2,$ and $x=3.$ Since the inequality is not strict, these values *are* solutions to the inequality.\nMoreover, these roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-2 \\right), \\qquad (-2,2), \\qquad (2,3),  \\qquad (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nOn each of these intervals, the factors of the polynomial are either always positive or always negative. We can find out which by filling a sign table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-2,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(3,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo fill out the sign table, we have to figure out the sign of each factor on each of the intervals. We can do this by finding the sign of each factor at a chosen test point in each of the intervals:\n\nIn the interval $(-\\infty, -2),$ we can choose the test point $x_1=-3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-3+2=-1<0, \\\\ x-2 & =-3-2=-5<0, \\\\ x-3 & =-3-3=-6<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso all three factors are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty, -2).$\n\nBy applying the same method for the other intervals and placing the signs in the sign table, we get the following table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-2,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(3,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo fill in the final row, we compute the product of the signs in each of the columns. This gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-2,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(3,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(2,3).$\n\nFinally, since this is an \"or equal to\" inequality, the roots of $f(x),$ namely $x=-2, x=2,$ and $x=3$ are also solutions to the inequality. Therefore, we have that $f(x) \\leq 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2] \\cup [2,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186135068 :tutorial/content "Suppose a rational function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=a$. Let's imagine that we're approaching the asymptote *from the left*, so $x\\to a^{-}$.\n\nThere are two possible behaviors that we can observe:\n\n- $f(x)$ *increases* without bound as $x$ approaches $a$ from the left. In this case, we write $f(x)\\to{\\color{blue}{+\\infty}}$ as $x\\to a^{-}$.\n- $f(x)$ *decreases* without bound as $x$ approaches $a$ from the left. In this case, we write $f(x)\\to{\\color{red}{-\\infty}}$ as $x\\to a^{-}$.\n\nWe can determine the unbounded behavior of rational functions near asymptotes by plotting them. However, plotting rational functions can be difficult.\n\nTo determine the behavior of a rational function near an asymptote *without* resorting to plots, we consider values of $f(x)$ close to the asymptote and deduce its behavior from there.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left.\n\nWe can determine how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left using a table of values like the one below.\n\n|  | Approaching $1$ (from the *left*) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$x$** | $0.9$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ | $0.9999$ | $\\cdots$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ | $-10\\,000$ | $\\cdots$ | undefined |\n|  | Decreasing to $\\color{red}-\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nAs we move to the left in our table,\n\n- the $x$-values get closer to $x=1$, and\n- the values of $f(x)$ are *negative*, and they *decrease* as we approach $x=1$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{red}{-\\infty}} \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow 1^-\n$$\n\n**Note**: In our table, we calculated the value of $f(x)$ at four points. After some practice, you'll only need to compute $f(x)$ at one or two points to determine its behavior near an asymptote." "Suppose a rational function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=a$. Let's imagine that we're approaching the asymptote *from the left*, so $x\\to a^{-}$.\n\nThere are two possible behaviors that we can observe:\n\n- $f(x)$ *increases* without bound as $x$ approaches $a$ from the left. In this case, we write $f(x)\\to{\\color{blue}{+\\infty}}$ as $x\\to a^{-}$.\n- $f(x)$ *decreases* without bound as $x$ approaches $a$ from the left. In this case, we write $f(x)\\to{\\color{red}{-\\infty}}$ as $x\\to a^{-}$.\n\nWe can determine the unbounded behavior of rational functions near asymptotes by plotting them. However, plotting rational functions can be difficult.\n\nTo determine the behavior of a rational function near an asymptote *without* resorting to plots, we consider values of $f(x)$ close to the asymptote and deduce its behavior from there.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$.\n- Therefore, $f(x)$ can approach *either* $\\color{blue}\\infty$ *or* $\\color{red}-\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left.\n\nWe can determine how $f(x)$ behaves as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left using a table of values like the one below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\">Approaching $1$ (from the <em>left</em>) $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$x$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9999$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10\\,000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\cdots$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">undefined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"5\" data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">Decreasing to $\\color{red}-\\infty$ $\\quad\\large\\longrightarrow$</td>\n<td data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAs we move to the left in our table,\n\n- the $x$-values get closer to $x=1$, and\n- the values of $f(x)$ are *negative*, and they *decrease* as we approach $x=1$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{red}{-\\infty}} \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow 1^-\n$$\n\n**Note**: In our table, we calculated the value of $f(x)$ at four points. After some practice, you'll only need to compute $f(x)$ at one or two points to determine its behavior near an asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186135144 :tutorial/content "In some cases, we might need to constrain the regression curve so that some of its coefficients equal zero. \n\nConsider the table below, which gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units. \n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $8$ | $18$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x=0.$ The company wishes to find a *quadratic* curve that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0).$\n\nSo, our regression curve must pass through the point $(0,0).$ In other words, the $y$-intercept of the quadratic curve must be $0.$ Therefore, we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/0f/0f98711850478ba6a152103452446e1b2a97ca64c2d47ab7c257db2d05335fcf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=5 \\\\ 3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=8 \\\\ 4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." "In some cases, we might need to constrain the regression curve so that some of its coefficients equal zero. \n\nConsider the table below, which gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Goods $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x=0.$ The company wishes to find a *quadratic* curve that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0).$\n\nSo, our regression curve must pass through the point $(0,0).$ In other words, the $y$-intercept of the quadratic curve must be $0.$ Therefore, we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/0f/0f98711850478ba6a152103452446e1b2a97ca64c2d47ab7c257db2d05335fcf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=5 \\\\ 3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=8 \\\\ 4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution."] [:db/cas 17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution."] [:db/cas 17592186136481 :answer/value "| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | **.** | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ |" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186136482 :answer/value "| **tens*